+---
+type: spec
+task_slug: parse-duration-trailing
+status: approved
+created: 2026-10-15
+tags: [timeparse, spec, python, bugfix, parsing]
+test_framework: pytest
+interview_rounds: 0
+lane: light
+summary: "parse_duration must reject text after the last unit instead of ignoring it"
+---
+
+# SPEC: parse_duration rejects trailing text
+
+## 🎯 Intent
+A user reported that `parse_duration("1h30m5")` returns 5400 because text after the last unit is silently ignored. Silently accepting malformed input hides caller bugs; the function's contract says anything else raises `ValueError`.
+
+## 🌅 Outcomes
+Any input with unconsumed text after the last unit raises `ValueError`. Every input accepted today that is well-formed keeps returning the same value.
+
+## 📋 In-Scope Scenarios
+
+### AC-001: Trailing text is rejected
+**Given** a duration string with valid units followed by extra text
+**When** `parse_duration` is called with `"1h30m5"`, `"1h30m!"`, `"2d junk"` or `"45sx"`
+**Then** it raises `ValueError` for each
+
+### AC-002: Documented valid inputs are unchanged
+**Given** well-formed inputs
+**When** `parse_duration` is called
+**Then** `" 1h30m "` -> 5400, `"1H30M"` -> 5400, `"0s"` -> 0, `"100000s"` -> 100000
+
+### AC-003: Leading or in-between junk still rejected
+**Given** text before or between units
+**When** `parse_duration` is called with `"x1h"` or `"1h 30m"`
+**Then** it raises `ValueError`
+
+## 🚫 Non-Goals
+- No change to unit set, ordering rule, or error messages.
+- No change to other modules (`format`, CLI).
+
+## ⚠️ Constraints
+| Constraint | Value | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Test framework | `pytest` | existing `tests/test_parse.py` |
+| Compatibility | public signature `parse_duration(text: str) -> int` unchanged | callers |
+
+## 🔒 Irreversible Decisions
+none
+
+## ✅ Verification Criteria
+| Scenario | Verification mode | Test name / manual step |
+|---|---|---|
+| AC-001 | unit | `test_rejects_trailing_text` |
+| AC-002 | unit | `test_valid_inputs_unchanged` |
+| AC-003 | unit | `test_rejects_leading_and_between_junk` |
+
+Oracle: golden for all three; evidence: values come verbatim from the bug report and the documented behaviours, not from the implementation.
+
+## ❓ Open Questions
+none
+
+## 🔍 Refinement Decisions
+- Not skipped (several scenarios, not title-obvious). Lane `light`: one file, one session, no irreversible decision. The ask fully specified the WHAT, so no interview round was needed.
+
+## 🔎 Spec Validation
+Advisory, single pass: `NEEDS_REVISION`. These findings do not gate approval.
+- **warning, acceptance:** no AC pins the existing ordering, repeat, unknown-unit or empty-input rejections (for example `"30m1h"`, `"1h1h"`, `"1z"`, `""`). A rewrite to a single `fullmatch` would pass every AC and silently drop the ordering rule. `/hm:execute` should add regression cases for these.
+- **warning, oracle:** `oracle_evidence` overstates provenance. Only `"1h30m5"` appears in the bug report. The other values are hand-computed from `UNITS` and the docstring.
+- **warning, irreversible:** the change narrows what a public function accepts, so inputs like `"10m!"` now raise. It is treated as a bug-fix correction of the documented "anything else raises ValueError" and left unlisted. The DRI may override this.
+- **suggestion:** `rejects_all` must require exactly `ValueError`. The "no change to error messages" non-goal has no check.
